  "activity": {
    "id": "activity_retrograde_motion",
    "name": "Apparent Retrograde Motion Lab",
    "description": "An interactive university-level astronomy lab exploring apparent retrograde motion. Students will compare geocentric and heliocentric models to understand how relative orbital velocities create the illusion of backward planetary movement.",
    "workplace": "workplace_astronomy_retrograde",
    "start": "step_1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Retrograde Puzzle",
          "description": "Observe the night sky above. Over six months, Mars typically drifts eastward against the background stars. However, every 26 months, it slows, reverses into a westward loop for about two months, and then resumes its eastward motion. This apparent retrograde motion was the central puzzle of pre-modern astronomy."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_sky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_night_sky",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_sky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_sky",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_geo"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_geo",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Geocentric Model: Epicycles",
          "description": "To explain the backward motion, ancient Ptolemaic astronomers placed Mars on a small circle (an epicycle) that rotated along a larger circle (the deferent) centered on Earth. Watch how this combined mechanical motion produces the retrograde loop, though it requires increasingly complex and non-parsimonious adjustments to match observation."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_earth",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_geocentric",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_earth",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_geo",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_earth",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_helio"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_helio",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Heliocentric Resolution",
          "description": "Now view the solar system from above. In the heliocentric model, Earth orbits at 1 AU (1 year period) and Mars at 1.52 AU (1.88 year period). Notice how Earth, moving faster on its inner track, overtakes Mars every 26 months. The complex retrograde loop vanishes\u2014replaced by simple, smooth elliptical orbits."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_heliocentric",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_helio",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_los"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_los",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Connecting the Views",
          "description": "Watch both perspectives simultaneously. A line of sight is drawn from Earth through Mars to the distant stars. As Earth overtakes Mars on the inside, the line briefly sweeps backward (westward). Retrograde motion is simply a parallax-like effect of relative velocity, much like passing a slower car on the highway."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_floor",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_line_of_sight",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_floor",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_los",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_floor",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_outer"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_outer",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Comparing the Outer Planets",
          "description": "Compare Mars to Jupiter (5.2 AU) and Saturn (9.5 AU). As distance from the Sun increases, the retrograde loops become narrower and less dramatic. This confirms that Earth's orbital motion subtends a smaller angle when viewing targets farther away, perfectly aligning with the geometry of a heliocentric system."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_outer_planets",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_outer",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_sun",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_6_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lab Complete",
          "description": "You have successfully explored retrograde motion. By comparing the awkward Ptolemaic epicycles to the elegant Copernican/Keplerian orbital mechanics, you have seen why the heliocentric model provides a simpler, more powerful framework for understanding planetary motion. Click to finish the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_sky",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "audio_recap",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_sky",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_6_recap"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
